<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moctezuma II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1466–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moctezuma II was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> from 1502 to 1520. He expanded the empire and centralised power, but faced the arrival of Hernan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> in 1519. Taken captive by the Spanish, he died in 1520 during the upheavals that preceded the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="Moctezuma II" width="261" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moctezuma_Mendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Moctezuma Xocoyotzin, called Moctezuma II in European sources because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> used no regnal numbers, was a son of the emperor Axayácatl and a daughter of Nezahualcóyotl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>. Two of his uncles, Tízoc and Ahuizotl, were the emperors before him. His name is interpreted as meaning he frowns like a lord. Most historians place his coronation in 1502, though some argue for 1503, a date that appears on the Stone of the Five Suns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his coronation Moctezuma set up thirty-eight more provincial divisions and sent out bureaucrats with military garrisons to collect tax, uphold the laws and act as local judges. His reign began with a drought in 1505 that lasted three years. He and the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> used the food supplies to feed the population and raised tribute for one year. Through warfare he extended the empire south to Xoconosco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-tehuantepec">Isthmus of Tehuantepec</a> and brought the Zapotec and Yopi peoples into it.</p><p>He widened the divide between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipiltin">pipiltin</a>, or nobles, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macehualtin">macehualtin</a>, or commoners, by barring commoners from working in the royal palaces. He also fought Huejotzingo and Tlaxcala, sometimes in flower wars, which were arranged by the parties to capture warriors for sacrifice. Many of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> around Tlaxcala were reluctant to fight their former allies, so his support there was limited.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1518 Moctezuma learned of the landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> of the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a> and ordered extra watchtowers on the coast. After the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> he became a prisoner in his own house; the sources do not make clear why. During the fighting after Cortés returned he was killed, probably on 29 June 1520, and accounts differ on how and by whom. The Spaniards fled the city and made a treaty with Tlaxcala to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><p>Portraits of him are divided. Spanish writers called him weak-willed and superstitious, and the historian James Lockhart suggests that he serves as a scapegoat for the defeat. Indigenous accounts of omens before the Spanish arrival are seen by many ethnohistorians as explanations made after the conquest. His name was later invoked in indigenous rebellions against Spanish rule, including in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> in 1721.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moctezuma II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Moctezuma II</b> (r. 1502–1520) tightened central control over tribute and appointments and intervened in the succession at Texcoco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1504, under <b>Moctezuma II</b>, the fighting grew fiercer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later, Zapotec and Mixtec artisans in Tenochtitlan made jewellery for Aztec rulers, including <b>Moctezuma II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Moctezuma II</b>’s palace, some 200 metres square, held law courts, a war council and a zoo.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Hernán Cortés and his Spanish and Tlaxcalan allies were received in the Aztec capital by <b>Moctezuma II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-8">8 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1501" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1872 1444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Moctezuma II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1501">What was here in 1501? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/axayacatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Axayacatl</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; r. 1469–1481</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Malinche, since then you repose no trust in me, take my son and my two legitimate daughters as hostages; only do not disgrace me, by demanding my person. What will the grandees of my empire say, if they see me taken prisoner?&#8221;</p><footer>To Cortés, on being seized in his palace, 1519 &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; as reported by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who was there &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alas, Malinche! why is it you wish to compel me to bring down total destruction on this town? Our gods are already angry with us, and who can tell what revenge they contemplate against you?&#8221;</p><footer>To Cortés, on his demand to take down the idols of the great temple, 1520 &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; as reported by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who was there &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why does Malinche now turn to me?—to me, who am tired of life, and who could wish never again to hear his name mentioned, for it is he who has plunged me into all this misery!&#8221;</p><footer>On being asked to calm his people, 1520 &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; as reported by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who heard it told &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
